/**

@page installation <h2>Installation</h2>

@todo figure out how to use footnotes. classical html methods seem not to work, and doxygen has no keyword for them

There are two ways to obtain Peano: 
You can either download one of the archives we provide on the webpage, or you
can work directly against a repository clone (recommended).
We recommend to work against the latest release of Peano which you can get through:

<pre>
git clone -b p4 https://gitlab.lrz.de/hpcsoftware/Peano.git
</pre>


in the directory where you've stored your downloaded file. 
If you work with the archive, type in

<code> <b> <br>
tar -xzvf myarchive.tar.gz<br>
</b></code> <br>
<br>
in the directory where you've stored your downloaded file.<br>
<br>
<h2> Prepare the configure environment</h2><br>
<br>
First, ensure you have the autotool packages installed on your system. They<br>
typically are shipped in the packages <code> <b>autoconf</b></code>, <code> <b>automake</b></code> and<br>
<code> <b>libtool</b></code>.<br>
Run the line below in the shell to set up the configure environment. These steps should only be required once, unless you push major revisions to the<br>
development branch.<br>
<br>
<code> <b> libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .; automake --add-missing; </b></code><br>
<br>
<h2> Configuration </h2><br>
The steps below will guide you through configuring and building the Peano code.<br>
An example of a shell script which summarises these steps can be found <br>
\ref sample_installation_script "here."<br>
<br>
<br>
Peano  relies on the autotools to set up its build environment.<br>
Change into the project's directory and type in: <br>
<br>
<code> <b>./configure --help</b></code><br>
<br>
The <code> <b> --help </b></code> option should give you some information about the available<br>
variants of Peano.<br>
A sole <code> <b>./configure</b></code><br>
call gives you the minimalist version of<br>
Peano, which might be appropriate for a first test.<br>
<br>
@remark <br>
I recommend that you start a first test without any additional flavours of<br>
Peano, i.e. to work with a plain <code> <b>./configure </b></code> call. Once the tests<br>
pass, I recommend that you add further options (IO) and/or parallelisation.<br>
Please note that the baseline config is not sufficient for most applications<br>
as it is merely a stripped down version of the Peano core.<br>
ExaHyPE for example requires you to add <code> <b>--enable-exahype</b></code>,<br>
<code> <b>--enable-loadbalancing-toolbox</b></code> and <code> <b>--enable-blockstructured</b></code>. <br>
But the bare/minimal version is good for first tests.<br>
<br>
The output of <code> <b>./configure --help</b></code> should be reasonably verbose, but I <br>
summarise some of the most important options below:<br>
<br>
<table> <br>
  <tr><br>
    <th><br>
      <code> <b> --with-multithreading </b> </code>
      <code> <b>  </b> </code>
    </th>

    <th>
      Switch on multithreading. By default, we
      build without multithreading, but using, for instance, <code> <b> --with-multithreading=omp </b> </code>
      makes Peano  use the OpenMP threading model. Please consult <code> <b> --help </b> </code> for
      details. Note: To use OpenMP multithreading, you will need a compiler which
      supports OpenMP 5.0 (e.g - GCC 9.x)[fn]]Peano realises its GPGPU
     offloading via OpenMP, too. Some compilers struggle to translate these GPGPU
      instructions. In this case, please configure with the CXXFLAG
     <code> <b> -DnoGPUOffloading </b> </code> .[/fn].
    </th>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <th>
      <code> <b> --with-mpi </b> </code>
      <code> <b>  </b> </code>
    </th>

    <th>
      Enable the MPI version of Peano. You have to tell the
      build environment however which compile command to use. Please note that
      we need a C++ MPI wrapper. So <code> <b>  --with-mpi=mpcxx</b> </code> is a typical call. Currently, you also need  <code> <b> --with-multithreading </b> </code> if you want to use MPI. 
    </th>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <th>
      <code> <b>--with-toolchain</b> </code>
    </th>

    <th>
      Build against a particular vendor toolchain.
      Currently, I have dedicated support for the NVIDIA NVTX SDK
      (<code> <b>--with-toolchain=nvidia</b> </code>) providing NVIDIA-specific logging/tracing
      as well as memory allocation calls---the latter is of use if you use
      GPUs---and the Intel toolchain (<code> <b>--with-toolchain=intel</b> </code>). The latter
      in particular links against the ITAC tracing API. I also have support for
      Google's Chrome browser (<code> <b>--with-toolchain=chrome</b> </code>) which means that
      traces are written in a way that you can display it in Chrome. Ensure that your CXXFLAGS set the right include path\footnote{It is
      <code> <b>CXXFLAGS+=-I/opt/nvidia/nsight-systems/2020.3.1/target-linux-x64/nvtx/include/nvtx3/</b> </code>
      on my system, e.g.}.
      This is optional.
    </th>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <th>
      <code> <b>--with-gpu</b> </code>
    </th>

    <th>
      Build against a particular vendor toolchain.
      Currently, I have dedicated support for the NVIDIA NVTX SDK
      (<code> <b>--with-toolchain=nvidia</b> </code>) providing NVIDIA-specific logging/tracing
      as well as memory allocation calls---the latter is of use if you use
      GPUs---and the Intel toolchain (<code> <b>--with-toolchain=intel</b> </code>). The latter
      in particular links against the ITAC tracing API. I also have support for
      Google's Chrome browser (<code> <b>--with-toolchain=chrome</b> </code>) which means that
      traces are written in a way that you can display it in Chrome. Ensure that your CXXFLAGS set the right include path\footnote{It is
      <code> <b>CXXFLAGS+=-I/opt/nvidia/nsight-systems/2020.3.1/target-linux-x64/nvtx/include/nvtx3/</b> </code>
      on my system, e.g.}.
      This is optional.
    </th>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <th>
      <code> <b>--enable-XXX</b> </code>
    </th>

    <th>
      A lot of frameworks or engines built on top of \Peano\
  have to be enabled explicitly. \ExaHyPE\ is probably the most popular engine
  shipped with the code, but there is also a particle/SPH module or some linear
  algebra stuff. Run with <code> <b>--help</b> </code> for a list of toolboxes. Some
  toolboxes have further internal and external dependencies, and the translation
  of the toolboxes can be time consuming. By default, \Peano\ thus disables all
  of its toolboxes.
    </th>
  </tr>

</table>


<!--  -->

Compilers, linkers and both compiler and linker flags can be changed by
resetting the corresponding environment variables (such as
<code> <b>CXX</b> </code> or <code> <b>LDFLAGS</b> </code> or <code> <b>CCFLAGS</b> </code>) \emph{prior} to the
configure call.
Alternatively, you can pass the variables to be used to <code> <b>configure</b> </code>
	through arguments (<code> <b>./configure CXX=icpc ...</b> </code>, e.g.). 
Please consult the <code> <b>--help</b> </code> output for details.
For some supercomputers that we use frequently, there are recommendations which
setting to use (Chapter~\ref{chapter:selected-HPC-platforms}).
@todo correct the above reference

@remark 
Most Linux tools prefer you to specify a dedicated installation directory via
<code> <b>--prefix</b> </code>.
For \Peano, things are slightly different: \Peano\ is a mere engine, i.e.~not
``useful'' per se.
It is applications built on top of \Peano\ that become tools that you might
later want to install/deploy on your system. 
Therefore, I ship \Peano\ with static libraries only, and most users work with
the code in the download/installation directory until they have constructed the
application with the help of \Peano.
So no need to specify a proper installation directory via <code> <b>--prefix</b> </code>.


<h2>Build </h2>
<code> <b> <br>
make<br>
</b> </code> 
should build the \Peano\ core.
It also builds some examples and all the extensions and toolboxes you have
manually enabled.
All of these go into static libraries.
The code is not small and you thus might prefer
<code> <b> <br>
make -jx<br>
</b> </code> 
with x being the number of threads to use for compilation.

@remark
Following up on the remark above, 
<code> <b> <br>
make install<br>
</b> </code> 
would deploy the \Peano\ files in the directory specified via the
<code> <b>prefix</b> </code> before.
Most Unix tools want you to install them. 
For \Peano, things might be slightly different as it is an engine used to
construct tools in the first place. 

<h2>Installation Test</h2>
Once you have compiled \Peano\, I recommend that you run all tests using

<code> <b> <br>
  make check<br>
</b> </code> 

The call launches all of \Peano's unit tests, i.e.~all the unit tests for the
core plus the extensions that you have enabled.

@remark

I have written the test target such that it works out-of-the-box for non-MPI
calls. If you have configured with <code> <b>--with-mpi</b> </code>, then the unit tests
will fail. But this does not mean that the unit tests are broken. It is simply
due to the fact that the makefile's test target does not know that you have to
use the <code> <b>mpirun</b> </code> prefix.
To make a long story short: If you use MPI and you want to run the unit tests,
you have to do so manually.

To run 2D and 3D tests individually, use

<code> <b> <br>
  src/unittests/UnitTests2d<br>
  src/unittests/UnitTests3d<br>
</b> </code> 

You can run these builds with different core counts and also MPI support if you
have compiled with MPI.
The executables contain both node correctness tests and MPI ping-pong tests,
i.e.~you can both assess a valid build plus a working MPI environment.
If successful, you should always get a message alike

<code> <b> <br>
Peano 4 (C) www.peano-framework.org <br>
build: 2d, with mpi (1 ranks), omp (4 threads), debug level=4<br>
it is recommended that you run the unit tests with more than one MPI rank, too (file:main.cpp,line:82)<br>
usage: ././UnitTests2d core-count (use default core count=1 now) (file:main.cpp,line:88)<br>
running test case collection "tarch.la.tests" ..... ok<br>
running test case collection "tarch.la" ............. ok<br>
running test case collection "tarch.mpi.tests" ..... ok<br>
running test case collection "tarch.mpi" ... ok<br>
running test case collection "tarch" ... ok<br>
running test case collection ... ok<br>
running test case collection "peano4.grid.tests" ....... ok<br>
running test case collection "peano4.grid" ... ok<br>
running test case collection "peano4.parallel.tests" ....... ok<br>
running test case collection "peano4.parallel" ... ok<br>
running test case collection "peano4" ... ok<br>
running test case collection ... ok<br>
</b> </code> 

Watch out for the last line that tells you whether the overall unit tests passed
through.

<h2> Python Configuration </h2>
\Peano\ is developed with Python 3. Python 2 is not supported. 


<h3> Python Paths</h3>
To make any Python example pass, you have to set the Python paths.
There are multiple ways to do so.

The standard way is to set the <code> <b>PYTHONPATH</b> </code>:
<code> <b> <br>
export PYTHONPATH=myPeanoDirectory/python<br>
</b> </code> 
This can even be done in the <code> <b>.bashrc</b> </code>.

Alternatively, you can augment your Python scripts later on with 
<code> <b> <br>
import sys<br>
PEANO4_PYTHON_DIR="myPeanoDirectory/python"<br>
sys.path.append(PEANO4_PYTHON_DIR)<br>
</b> </code> 

Please note that most Python scripts require you to set the
<code> <b>LD\_LIBRARY\_PATH</b> </code>. 
If you install \Peano, then this path should be automatically right.
If you work with a local \Peano\ installation, you can either tell \Peano\
through its API where to search for the library (all routines that add a
library accept additional search paths), or you can again use the
<code> <b>sys.path.append</b> </code> command to extend <code> <b>LD\_LIBRARY\_PATH</b> </code>.

@remark
I personally prefer the variant to go through the environment variable.
\Peano's Python API yield plain old makefiles building executables that work
completely without any Python. 
This way, we ensure that we work smoothly on supercomputers, too, where Python
sometimes is not available in the latest version or on the compute nodes.
As long as you run through environment variables or the \Peano\ routines to
specify paths, your makefiles also will continue to work that way. 

*/



